{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"smart-disloyal-naga","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Discovered from\n\nrebar-debug on bug `masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` (3a52-2d79-320d-4fed), root cause RC-A. See\nthat ticket's \"Phase 1 EXIT\" comment for the full evidence trail.\n\n## Problem\n\n`src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:99` calls `materialize_tickets(...)` on every attested gate\nresolution. `materialize_tickets` (`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:675-703`)\ncontent-addresses the entry as `<store>/tickets-<sha>` and builds it with `_materialize_tree`\n(`:373-410`, `git read-tree` + `git checkout-index`) — every file written fresh, with no\nhardlinking, no delta against a neighbouring entry, and no per-review release\n(`SnapshotHandle._cleanup` at `:199` is never assigned by any producer).\n\nThe key is the live tickets-branch tip: `_pin_tickets_sha` (`:534-609`) resolves the tracker\nvia `config.tracker_dir()`, and `tracker_dir_override()` (`src/rebar/_config_sources.py:65-79`)\nreturns `REBAR_TRACKER_DIR` verbatim.\n\nMeasured on `origin/tickets`:\n\n- **69,984 blobs**, ≈620 MiB (~800 MiB allocated). The code snapshot alongside it is\n  41.6 MiB / 3,131 files — **15x smaller**.\n- **1,374 commits** in a 10-hour window (2026-08-17 18:00 → 2026-08-18 04:00 PDT): the tip\n  moves every ~26 seconds.\n- each of the last 5 consecutive tickets commits changed **exactly 1 file**.\n\nSo a one-file delta produces a fresh 620 MiB copy — roughly 10^5x write amplification — on a\nkey with effectively **zero reuse**. The review-bot's own `code_review` artifact write\n(`REBAR_SYNC_PUSH: always`, `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:120`) guarantees the ref advances\nbetween reviews even if nothing else in the fleet were writing.\n\nOn the review-bot host this modelled 2.5-10 GiB/h of root-disk growth; the measured rate during\nthe 2026-08-17 incident was 4.4 GiB/h.\n\n## Why this is a defect\n\n`docs/adr/0005-snapshot-cache-architecture.md` D2 rests on \"an immutable SHA is a perfect cache\nkey\", which holds only for keys with **reuse**. This key has none, so the structure behaves as\nan append-only log of full copies rather than the bounded, reclaimable cache the ADR describes.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A review no longer writes a full copy of the ticket tree when the previous entry differs\n      by a handful of files (hardlink/reflink against the prior entry, materialize only the\n      paths the gate actually reads, or pin a coarser/stabler key) — with the chosen approach\n      justified against ADR 0005's integrity guarantees.\n- [ ] A test observes the amplification RED (N reviews across N adjacent tickets SHAs consume\n      ~1 tree, not N trees) and GREEN after the repair.\n- [ ] `materialize_tickets` participates in the store's byte accounting (see the sibling\n      ticket) so the entries it creates are visible to any size-based reclaim.\n- [ ] ADR 0005 / `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md` updated to state the key-reuse assumption and how\n      the tickets key satisfies it.","id":"8386-a512-4815-4e6b","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Gate snapshot store copies the whole ~620 MiB ticket tree per review on a cache key with no reuse"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787173719217463001,"uuid":"d78987c7-fa93-471b-b554-6ec041ab3f47"}